Lab 12: Infrastructure Security Best Practices
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Implement network segmentation for management, storage, and tenant traffic in OpenStack.
Configure firewalls and TLS encryption for secure inter-service communication.
Set up multi-factor authentication (MFA) for admin access.
Prerequisites
A deployed OpenStack environment (e.g., DevStack or production-grade deployment).
Administrative access to the OpenStack controller node.
Basic familiarity with Linux commands and OpenStack CLI.
openstack-client and python-openstackclient packages installed.
A valid SSL/TLS certificate (self-signed or CA-signed) for encryption.
Task 1: Implement Network Segmentation
Subtask 1.1: Create Segregated Networks
Objective: Separate management, storage, and tenant traffic into distinct networks.

Create Management Network:

openstack network create --provider-network-type flat --provider-physical-network physnet1 management
openstack subnet create --network management --subnet-range 192.168.1.0/24 --gateway 192.168.1.1 management-subnet
Create Storage Network:

openstack network create --provider-network-type vlan --provider-physical-network physnet1 --provider-segment 101 storage
openstack subnet create --network storage --subnet-range 10.0.1.0/24 --gateway 10.0.1.1 storage-subnet
Create Tenant Network:

openstack network create --provider-network-type vxlan tenant
openstack subnet create --network tenant --subnet-range 172.16.0.0/24 --gateway 172.16.0.1 tenant-subnet
Expected Outcome:
Three isolated networks appear in openstack network list.

Troubleshooting:

Verify physical networks (physnet1) exist in openstack network agent list.
Ensure VLAN/VXLAN is supported by the underlying infrastructure.
Subtask 1.2: Apply Security Groups
Objective: Restrict traffic flow between networks.

Create Security Groups:

openstack security group create management-sg --description "Management traffic only"
openstack security group create storage-sg --description "Storage traffic only"
Add Rules:

# Allow SSH (port 22) and API traffic (port 5000) for management
openstack security group rule create --proto tcp --dst-port 22 management-sg
openstack security group rule create --proto tcp --dst-port 5000 management-sg

# Allow NFS (port 2049) and iSCSI (port 3260) for storage
openstack security group rule create --proto tcp --dst-port 2049 storage-sg
openstack security group rule create --proto tcp --dst-port 3260 storage-sg
Expected Outcome:
Traffic is restricted to designated ports per network.

Task 2: Configure Firewalls and TLS Encryption
Subtask 2.1: Enable TLS for API Endpoints
Objective: Encrypt communication between OpenStack services.

Modify nova.conf:

[ssl]
enabled = true
cert_file = /etc/ssl/certs/nova-cert.pem
key_file = /etc/ssl/private/nova-key.pem
ca_file = /etc/ssl/certs/ca.pem
Restart Services:

sudo systemctl restart nova-api
Expected Outcome:
curl -vk https://<controller-ip>:8774 shows TLS handshake success.

Subtask 2.2: Configure Firewall (iptables)
Objective: Harden the controller node.

Allow Essential Ports:

sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT    # SSH
sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -j ACCEPT   # HTTPS
sudo iptables -A INPUT -j DROP                        # Deny all else
Persist Rules:

sudo apt install iptables-persistent
sudo netfilter-persistent save
Troubleshooting:
Test connectivity before dropping traffic (iptables -L -v).

Task 3: Set Up Multi-Factor Authentication
Subtask 3.1: Install and Configure FreeOTP
Objective: Enable MFA for OpenStack Dashboard (Horizon).

Install libpam-google-authenticator:

sudo apt install libpam-google-authenticator
**Configure PAM**: Edit /etc/pam.d/sshd`:

auth required pam_google_authenticator.so
Enforce MFA in Horizon: Modify /etc/openstack-dashboard/local_settings.py:

WEBSSO_ENABLED = True
WEBSSO_CHOICES = [("mfa", "Multi-Factor Auth")]
Expected Outcome:
Admins must enter a OTP after password login.

Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Segregated OpenStack networks for security.
Enforced TLS encryption and firewall rules.
Implemented MFA for administrative access.
Validation:

Verify network isolation: openstack network show <network-name>.
Test TLS: openssl s_client -connect <controller-ip>:8774.
Confirm MFA: Attempt dashboard login with OTP.
Next Steps:
Explore integrating LDAP for centralized authentication or auditing with OpenStack Telemetry (Ceilometer).
